{"prompt": "Explain how a DNS lookup works in 80 to 100 words.", "text": "A DNS lookup translates a domain name into an IP address. Your device first checks local caches, then queries a recursive resolver. The resolver asks a root server, which points to the appropriate top-level domain server (like .com). That server directs it to the domain's authoritative nameserver, which returns the correct IP address. The resolver caches the result and sends it back to your device, which then connects to the website. This entire process typically happens in milliseconds, often without you noticing.", "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 18, "total_tokens": 119, "completion_tokens": 101}, "finish_reason": "stop", "requested_max_tokens": 160, "total_seconds": 12.127736716000072, "ttft_seconds": 1.357188825000776, "overall_tokens_per_second": 8.328017202645167, "first_content_event_tokens": 1, "content_events": 43, "decode_tokens_per_second": 9.284578743070977}
